#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572774} :activity/id #uuid "f24b3b44-c729-5176-9a67-9bc09288acd6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048993 :topic/id #uuid "d1b21eb7-a767-5b1f-8e65-566b74a26f39" :topic/math-academy-id 2552} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572691 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a10968a-1243-571b-a9bd-8c6631c847bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u=-\\frac{1}{2}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572345} {:answer/id #uuid "440df762-731d-56df-bc2f-7e0b6dfe87dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u=\\frac{1}{5}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572398} {:answer/id #uuid "81b9413e-71d2-55bc-b120-48cf7418e25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u=-5z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572455} {:answer/id #uuid "bbcec67a-21f7-515b-b67b-d4dfa9b9a0fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u=5z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572525} {:answer/id #uuid "c2fd7900-c51f-5bed-9b31-6871651fb51e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u=-\\frac{1}{5}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572535}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572535} :answer-field/id #uuid "35a7e094-9492-556b-b055-0b4284ba502d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "528c6dbc-6dbe-5499-88b0-b6881a756455" :question/math-academy-id "q-115639" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9e3a9bc6a2c642697cfa2b6525e3abf42a0227f0ac66eecade156a842fb5e92d.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a direct variation between the variables $u$ and $z.$ Which equation describes the relationship between the variables?" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ varies directly with $z,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nu=kz.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice there is a point on the graph with $z=-10$ and $u=2.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =k\\cdot (-10) \\\\ -\\frac{2}{10} & =k \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nu=-\\frac{1}{5}z.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "35310a5a-f14e-5220-a85f-b4ffe8767295"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d37e1bb-efc8-5170-a04c-6bd70009b86b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ dollars per pound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572350} {:answer/id #uuid "2f1efd6d-9c98-5e26-81d8-cffc494aafcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ dollars per pound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572372} {:answer/id #uuid "3b7818b8-beef-5b29-af60-15faef7c0a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ dollars per pound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572387} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0a3f95-e65f-5423-819f-da1d3c12223c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{2}{3}$ dollars per pound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572407} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0d083c-0799-5884-af03-185f6861a496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ dollars per pound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572387} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c911119-b189-59c9-afe1-208ee09fedb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d9eeaf2b-c92c-529e-9218-24a01a5754ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-68193" :question/problem "![](images/25/2501f7e346abf6c79a211fd090162be685c001bc6acc68787d8c73c0eee7a8af.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a proportional relationship between the weight $x$ (in pounds) of ice cream sold by a store and the profit $y$ (in dollars).  What is the constant of proportionality?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(3,4).$\n\n![](images/7b/7beacc5836ed16a4dcb36916d0273513b7b2d94871c4ee5994d56479bc11f18d.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}=1\\,\\text{R}1=1\\,\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ dollars per pound."} :step/id #uuid "36a5066a-12cf-5071-9d56-323a5a484789" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1746c645-2ca1-5886-a22d-da171cd15a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "L=50t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572342} {:answer/id #uuid "4e190a43-d3de-5ac0-b00b-dc513b13624d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "L=60t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572411} {:answer/id #uuid "56acae82-639f-5413-88a9-a7b04e1a0938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "L=90t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572418} {:answer/id #uuid "b3983d19-ed17-57aa-997b-ec527346494e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "L=30t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572517} {:answer/id #uuid "ea1c18ae-83f8-5cdc-a1af-f2cbf0066c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "L=15t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572570} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf78e6b5-1138-5983-a5e7-632caf3ecb6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eabcc9c8-1fae-5b8f-9d65-4f29f12ab5ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-51883" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa895816cc537fc2ccb30f99f2f9c8ec01cf0ee75ba76fc8a2aed1c0b3349206.png)\n\nThe graph above shows that the volume of gas $L$ in a truck's tank is directly proportional to the time $t$ since the driver started filling it. Find a direct variation equation that describes the relationship between $L$ and $t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $L$ varies directly with $t,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nL=kt.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice that there is a point on the graph with $t=6$ and $L=90.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90 & =k\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\frac{90}{6} & =k \\\\ 15 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nL=15t.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7d66a73b-7d01-5490-b561-23c518b44d9a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572750}}] :activity/title "Understanding Proportional Relationships Using Graphs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572316}]}
